WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.270
A student wrote a function to

2
00:00:02.270 --> 00:00:03.570
count down from three.

3
00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:06.300
It called itself, and called

4
00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:09.080
itself, and never stopped, until

5
00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:10.880
Python stopped it with an error.

6
00:00:11.480 --> 00:00:14.360
One line was missing: the base case.

7
00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:17.830
Today: recursion, functions

8
00:00:17.830 --> 00:00:19.190
that call themselves.

9
00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:22.670
A recursive function has two parts.

10
00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:25.800
The base case answers at once,

11
00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:27.260
without calling itself.

12
00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:30.280
The recursive case calls the same

13
00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:32.000
function on a smaller problem.

14
00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:36.940
Factorial of n is n times factorial of n

15
00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:40.240
minus one, and factorial of one is one.

16
00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:42.800
Follow fact of four.

17
00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.120
It waits for fact of three, which

18
00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:48.760
waits for fact of two, down to

19
00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:50.660
fact of one, the base case.

20
00:00:51.340 --> 00:00:53.700
Then the answers come back up: one,

21
00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:56.380
two, six, and twenty-four.

22
00:00:57.580 --> 00:00:59.940
Every waiting call sits on the call

23
00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:02.260
stack, and the stack has limits.

24
00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:05.730
Without a base case, the calls pile

25
00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:07.350
up until Python stops them.

26
00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:10.150
On the phone that’s after about a

27
00:01:10.150 --> 00:01:11.510
hundred and fifty calls.

28
00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:13.790
Add the base case, and the

29
00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:15.030
countdown stops at zero.

30
00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:18.730
Recursion shines when a problem

31
00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:20.810
holds smaller copies of itself.

32
00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:24.550
To move a tower of three discs, move the

33
00:01:24.550 --> 00:01:26.990
top two out of the way, move the biggest,

34
00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:29.110
then move the two back on top.

35
00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:30.710
That’s seven moves.

36
00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:34.520
Four discs take fifteen: each extra

37
00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.680
disc doubles the work, plus one.

38
00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:38.490
Quick check!

39
00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:42.880
A recursive function has no base case.

40
00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:45.210
What happens when you run it?

41
00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:48.820
It keeps calling itself until the

42
00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:51.020
call stack runs out, and Python

43
00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:52.160
stops it with an error.

44
00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:54.740
So every recursive function

45
00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:56.020
needs a base case.

46
00:01:56.830 --> 00:01:59.110
And every call must move closer

47
00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:01.250
to it, or it never arrives.

48
00:02:02.630 --> 00:02:04.770
Recursion can also waste work.

49
00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:07.420
Each Fibonacci number is the

50
00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:09.100
sum of the two before it.

51
00:02:09.670 --> 00:02:12.730
Written the obvious way, fib of four

52
00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:15.230
works out fib of two twice.

53
00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:18.010
Fib of forty makes hundreds

54
00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:19.590
of millions of calls.

55
00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:22.770
The fix is to remember answers.

56
00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:26.240
Keep a dictionary of results, and look an

57
00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:28.220
answer up before working it out.

58
00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:32.080
Now fib of forty takes about eighty calls

59
00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:33.960
instead of hundreds of millions.

60
00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:36.340
That’s called memoisation.

61
00:02:37.580 --> 00:02:39.480
In the app, open Recursion.

62
00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:43.000
Step through factorial’s calls, and count

63
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.100
how much work Fibonacci repeats.

64
00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:48.320
Then solve the Towers of Hanoi with

65
00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:50.420
four discs in the fewest moves.

66
00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:52.550
Your turn.

67
00:02:53.170 --> 00:02:56.010
Fib of thirty is painfully slow,

68
00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:58.370
because it works out the same

69
00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:00.210
values again and again.

70
00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:02.200
What fixes it best?

71
00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:05.640
Remember the answers in a dictionary.

72
00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:09.570
In short: a recursive function needs a

73
00:03:09.570 --> 00:03:12.310
base case and a smaller recursive case.

74
00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:15.110
Each waiting call sits on the

75
00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:17.230
call stack, which has limits.

76
00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:20.730
And when work repeats, memoise.

77
00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:23.810
Try it yourself: write a

78
00:03:23.810 --> 00:03:25.310
recursive sum of a list.

79
00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:28.830
An empty list sums to zero; otherwise,

80
00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:31.530
it’s the first item plus the sum of the

81
00:03:31.530 --> 00:03:31.830
rest.

82
00:03:32.420 --> 00:03:34.620
Trace it on paper with three items,

83
00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:36.880
then test it on an empty list.
